    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Breakdown and Comparison\n\nGoogle uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for initial equity grants (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** over 4 years). \n\nHere is how the Google offer compares to your current Meta compensation:\n\n| Component | Meta (IC6 Current) | Google (L6 Offer - Year 1) | Google (L6 4-Yr Annual Avg) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 *(15%)* | $47,000 *(20%)* | $47,000 *(20%)* |\n| **Equity** | ~$250,000/yr | **$356,400** *(33% of $1.08M)* | $270,000/yr *(if flat avg)* |\n| **Sign-on Bonus**| N/A | $0 | $0 |\n| **Total Year 1 TC** | **~$526,000** | **~$638,400** | **~$552,000/yr** |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Offer Assessment\n\n* **Equity ($1.08M):** **Strong.** For an L6 PM in the Bay Area, initial equity grants typically range from $750k to $1M+. $1.08M is in the upper quartile of standard initial bands.\n* **Base Salary ($235k):** **Low.** Google\u2019s stated band goes up to $271,000. Taking a $5,000 pay cut on base salary when you were headhunted makes no sense.\n* **Sign-on Bonus ($0):** **Very weak.** Passive candidates recruited from Meta IC6 roles almost always receive a sign-on bonus to offset unvested equity transitions, transition friction, and timing risks around bonus cycles.\n* **The \"Year 3 Cliff\":** Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting means equity drops to 22% in Year 3 and 12% in Year 4. While annual refreshers help cushion this, as the recruiter noted, refreshes depend on performance ratings and company budgets.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Verdict: Negotiate\n\n**Do not accept as-is, and do not walk away yet.** \n\nYou hold maximum leverage:\n1. Google reached out to you.\n2. You are happy and established at Meta as an IC6.\n3. You have substantial unvested equity and performance track record where you are.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\nTo make the transition compelling, target **~$720k+ Year 1 Total Comp**:\n\n1. **Base Salary:** Ask for **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000**.\n   * *Rationale:* You should not take a base salary reduction, and $255k\u2013$260k sits comfortably within their published $183k\u2013$271k band.\n2. **Sign-on Bonus:** Ask for **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000**.\n   * *Rationale:* Compensates for giving up your upcoming Meta bonus/equity cadence and makes the first-year upside clear.\n3. **Equity (GSUs):** Ask for **$1,200,000 \u2013 $1,250,000** over 4 years.\n   * *Rationale:* L6 PM equity ceilings can reach $1.2M\u2013$1.3M for competitive candidates.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Framing & Script\n\nFrame your negotiation around **risk, career continuity, and total opportunity cost**, not greed.\n\n#### Sample Script / Email:\n\n> *\"Thank you for extending this offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team throughout the interview process and can see a strong fit for my background.*\n> \n> *As mentioned early on, I wasn\u2019t actively looking and am in a strong position at Meta with substantial unvested equity and strong performance standing. To make leaving my current role compelling, I would need the offer adjusted in a few areas:*\n> \n> 1. * **Base Salary:** Since my current base is $240,000, I would like to increase the base to **$255,000** (well within your posted range).*\n> 2. * **Sign-on Bonus:** To offset leaving my upcoming vesting and bonus cycle, I\u2019d like to include a sign-on bonus of **$75,000**.*\n> 3. * **Equity:** To ensure long-term comp upside given the Year 3/4 vesting structure, I would like to bring the equity grant to **$1,200,000**.*\n> \n> *If we can bridge the gap to these numbers, I would be thrilled to sign and join the team.\"*",
