     "content": "### The Short Answer\n\n**Yes, you should strongly consider accepting this offer.** \n\nFinancially, this is an exceptional outcome. While they held firm on the sign-on bonus, increasing the equity grant from **$1.08M to $1.28M** added **$200,000 in equity**, effectively folding the sign-on compensation directly into your first two years of vesting.\n\nAt **$1.28M equity**, you are at the top tier of Google\u2019s L6 Product Manager compensation band.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Year-by-Year Financial Breakdown\n\nBecause Google vests initial grants **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**, your actual payout is heavily front-loaded:\n\n| Component | Meta (Current) | Google Year 1 | Google Year 2 | Google Year 3 *(pre-refresh)* |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Bonus** | ~$36,000 *(15%)* | $48,000 *(20%)* | $48,000 *(20%)* | $48,000 *(20%)* |\n| **Equity Vesting** | ~$250,000 | **$422,400** *(33%)* | **$422,400** *(33%)* | **$281,600** *(22%)* |\n| **Total Comp (TC)** | **~$526,000** | **$710,400** | **$710,400** | **$569,600 + Refreshes** |\n\n#### Key Financial Takeaways:\n* **Immediate Pay Bump:** You gain **+$184,400/year (+35%)** in each of your first two years compared to your Meta run-rate.\n* **Offsetting Unvested Equity:** In Year 1 alone, your Google equity ($422,400) is **$222,400 higher** than the $200,000 in Meta RSUs you would walk away from. You make up for the forfeited equity in your first 12 months.\n* **The Year 3/4 Cliff:** Year 3 baseline equity drops to $281,600, but by then you will have accumulated annual equity refreshes from Years 1 and 2, which will keep your Year 3 TC around **$620k\u2013$660k+** (assuming standard performance).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why Google Held Firm on Sign-On\n\nGoogle compensation committees frequently trade cash sign-ons for equity bumps when pushed to the top of a level. By raising your equity to $1.28M, they gave you an additional **~$66,000/year in cash-equivalent value in both Year 1 and Year 2**, which matches or exceeds the $75,000 sign-on bonus you would have otherwise received.\n\nPushing further on compensation at this stage carries diminishing returns and risks offer fatigue, as $1.28M RSU is already an exception-level grant for an L6 PM.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Non-Financial Factors to Make Your Decision\n\nSince the money is clearly there, your decision should come down to career trajectory and work environment:\n\n1. **Scope & Team Impact:**\n   * Is the Google SF team working on a core tier-1 product or an exploratory initiative? \n   * Meta PMs generally have more autonomous execution authority, whereas Google PMs often navigate broader cross-functional consensus and stakeholder alignment.\n2. **Promotion Path (L7 / IC7):**\n   * Meta IC6 $\\rightarrow$ IC7 and Google L6 $\\rightarrow$ L7 are both notoriously difficult plateaus. If you are close to an IC7 promotion at Meta, switching resets your timeline by at least 1.5\u20132 years. If you are not close to IC7 at Meta, starting fresh with a $710k floor at Google is a strong strategic move.\n3. **Commute / Lifestyle:**\n   * Transitioning to Google's San Francisco office versus your current setup at Meta.\n\n---\n\n### Final Recommendation\n\nIf you like the team and product area at Google, **accept the offer**. \n\nYou successfully pushed them to match your base, raised total compensation by over $184,000/year for your first two years, and secured a top-of-band $1.28M equity package.",
